========================================================================
4. POWER -- would a clock of the published strength have been seen?
========================================================================
  standard error of the slope      : 3.799e-05
  minimum slope detectable at 80%  : 1.069e-04 substitutions/site/year
  published genus rate mu          : 1.000e-05
  ratio mu / minimum detectable    : 0.09

  power to detect a slope of exactly mu : 5.6%

  --- how to read this ---
  Power is 5.6%. A clock running at the published genus
  rate would NOT reliably have been detected in these data, so the
  absent signal is consistent with that rate rather than evidence
  against it. This is the result that makes the negative finding
  informative: say that the data cannot resolve a clock of this
  strength, not that no clock exists.

  CAVEAT, which must be stated wherever this number is reported:
  root-to-tip distances are not independent -- tips sharing ancestry
  share branches -- so the effective sample size is below n and the
  true standard error is larger than the one above. This power estimate
  is therefore an UPPER bound, and the real power is lower. That makes
  a low-power verdict safe and a high-power verdict provisional.

========================================================================
5. WHAT THE STANDING DIVERSITY CORRESPONDS TO IN TIME
========================================================================
  within-LSDV diversity pi         : 4.870e-03
  pi / 2mu                         : 244 years
  pi across the alignment          : 537 pairwise differences

  Two mean pairwise differences of 537 substitutions at 1.10
  substitutions per year place the common ancestor of sampled LSDV
  diversity on the order of 244 years ago.
  This is a back-of-envelope conversion under a strict clock and
  constant population size, neither of which holds here. Report it as
  an order of magnitude, with those assumptions named, or not at all.

